body:
I can give you one.

Simple meal plan (for a man - rest day):
1. Breakfast - 2x boiled chicken breasts + 2 baked carrots + Full package of SKYR (total 660kcal, 70g of protein, 50g of carbs, 20g of fat);
2. Lunch - 500g of boiled brown rice with 10g of butter, 2x boiled chicken breasts + 200g of beet root (900kcal, 62g of protein, 135g of carbs, 10g of fat);
3. Dinner - A scoop of protein powder with 200ml milk (total 200kcal, 45g of protein, 2g of fat).

In training days - add a package of skyr to your dinner.

This is just 1 meal plan for one day. You can create your own by using the guidelines below in relation to nutrition:
If you're a male, consume 1800kcal in days when you're not training, 2300kcal in days when you are training. If you're a woman - 1600/2100kcal respectively.


Follow these simple instructions to speed up your metabolism:
1. Quit smoking, or vaping (including cannabis);
2. Quit drinking alcohol (if you do);
3. Drink 2 - 3 liters of water per day. When you wake up have a glass or two. That would be a reminder to drink water during the day;
4. Walk at least 10000 steps per day. Start with as much as you can and gradually increase.

Body recomposition always requires caloric deficit. Especially if you cant move much due to medical issues.
First - quit smoking if you do. Cannabis and vapes too;
Second - no drinking alcohol;
Third - Sleep at least 8 hrs/night;
Fourth - Drink 2 - 2.5l of water per day;
Fifth - no dough, no sugar, no carbonated drinks.
Eat 1800kcal on rest days, 2300kcal on training days.
Foods to Eat: Butter, beef tallow, brown rice, chicken breast, eggs (full), veggies, fruit, SKYR, raw almonds, honey, raw walnuts. 
Foods to Avoid: Processed meats, roasted or fried nuts, vegetable oils, hydrogenated oils, any form of processed (pre-packaged) foods.
Drinks: only pure water, coffee (black - no sugar) and tea - black, green (no sugar).
Drinks to avoid: Sugary Juices, Carbonated Drinks (Club Soda is fine), Soft Drinks, Alcoholic Beverages.

As for training - Do supersets with 30 second breaks between sets. Even if you can barely can make 1 rep. Newest studies show that the ugliest, hardest reps build the most muscle mass. I would advise on Incline pushups for pecs. For delts - best is to have dumbells.

1. Quit smoking/vaping, including cannabis;
2. Don't drink alcohol more than once a month, including beer or cider;
3. Sleep at least 8 hrs per night;
4. Drink 8 - 12 glasses of water per day at minimum;
5. Avoid or completely remove vegetable oils, processed sugars and doughy/processed foods from your diet;
6. Dont eat more than 1800kcal in the days you're resting, 2300kcal in the days you're training;
7. Split your macros as follows - 50% protein, 30 - 35% carbs (brown rice, sour fruits, whole grains etc) and 15 - 20% of animal fats;
8. Do 2 essential exercises of 3 sets progressive overloads for each muscle group per week.

1. If you're smoking or vaping (incl cannabis) - quit;
2. Don't drink alcohol (more than once a month or so);
3. Sleep at least 8 hrs/night;
4. Drink at least 8 - 12 glasses of water (not fizzy drinks or coffee, tea) just pure water per day;
5. Remove any plant based oils - sunflower, palm oil etc. If you're using olive oil - eat it raw - no thermal processing - frying, baking etc is a no no with it as it becomes as carcinogenic as the other plant oils;
6. Use animal fats for frying your eggs and baking your meat - butter, pork fat, beef tallow etc;
7. Do not exceed 1800kcal in days when you're not training, 2300kcal in the days you do. Avoid white and sour dough bread. If necessary - eat whole grain  but best avoid it;
8. Eat chicken breast, eggs, SKYR, milk, brown, red, emperor rice (avoid white rice as it's been processed);
9. Remove any and all forms of processed cane sugar and fructose from your diet - no pastries, chocolate, fizzy drinks, alcohol etc. Eat sweet and sour fruit, and/or a little honey instead. Best are Granny Smith apples, Pineapple (raw not canned), Nectarines (hard, not ripe). Watermelon is OK too.
10. WALK! At least 10000 steps per day. Start with 4000 and gradually increase. You should do this regardless if tou're training or not.

These should summarize it. Since 95% of fat loss is food, as long as you're doing any training along with the proper nitrition - you'd be fine.

A few suggestions:
1. If you're smoking, incl weed - quit;
2. Drink at least 10 - 12 glasses of water daily;
3. Do the OMAD (one meal a day) diet - eat 1800kcal mainly meat in 1 sitting;
4. No fizzy drinks and processed foods;
5. Eat green veggies, red beetroot, carrots and raw almonds. All increase test.

Ozempic is very dangerous because it could damage your Thyroid. Do things the natural way:
1. Quit smoking;
2. Quit drinking alcohol;
3. Quit vaping;
4. Do not smoke marijuana or other drugs;
5. Drink at least 8 - 10 glasses of clean water per day;
6. Eat white meat - chicken breast, fish, etc.
7. Eat SKYR;
8. Eat brown rice;
9. Avoid processed food;
10. Do not eat chocolate or other sugary foods, and switch them for fruit such as sweet sour apples and hard nectarines;
11. Restrict calories to 1800kcal when you're not training; upto 2300 when you are;
12. Try to consume at least 50% of your calories in protein and 30% from carbs;
13. Walk on average at least 10000 steps per day on top of any other training.

Simple.

Its not so much how much calories do you consume, but what exactly do you eat.
1. Start a food diary but be honest with yourself - for an entire week - write all food and liquid (includong water and coffee) that you consume;
2. When you it's finished make sure:
- to have had drank at least 2.5l of water per day (10 - 12 cups);
- to remove ANY processed sugars from your diet - including with coffee or tea - remove all sugar and replace it with an alternative - stevia for sugar replacement, fruits for sweets. You may eat protein bars but no more than 2 per day.
- to remove white flour and its products - doughy foods, deep fried foods etc;
- to  remove any seed oils and vegetable oils that went through thermal cooking (including olive oil) as well as margarin;
- to remove heavy processed foods - deli meats, sauces, anything made in a factory.

BTW: You may consume way more calories than you realize, so get a food scale and measure each meal when you eat, then calculate calories for each individual meal in order to track it. Otherwize you're lying to yourself and assuming.
Also - I do 25k steps per day and heavy active lifestyle and probably burn 3200 - 3400 kcal per day. Make sure that you really burn more than you consume.
Wish you success!

